title open problem 6: one rename composes, two at once do not

# Section 15, open problem 6.  Substitution recognizes a rename only
# when a pool lost exactly one name and gained exactly one: with one
# of each there is a single correspondence to draw, so it is drawn.
# Rename two links of the same pool in one go and there are two ways
# to pair the losses with the gains.  Nothing in the snapshots
# separates them -- the two names are literally the same bytes, and a
# full path carries no ordering -- so succession declines to guess and
# both gained names degrade to attachments with no antecedent.
#
# The two folders below differ by exactly one rename, and that is the
# only thing that differs.  One image, four names, in both.  Onto
# un-shares hero.png from the other three in both.
#
# one-rename/ -- off-of renames banner.png to masthead.png and leaves
#   the rest alone.  One loss, one gain: masthead.png is read through
#   banner.png, inherits banner's place in onto's split, and the
#   rename composes.  Two pools out, the renamed name in the right
#   one.  This is the answer everybody wants.
#
# two-renames/ -- off-of renames banner.png to masthead.png AND
#   header.png to topbar.png.  Two losses, two gains, no
#   correspondence, so both new names arrive as attachments.  An
#   attachment links to the pool it landed in (L3), and that link now
#   runs between splash.png and hero.png -- the two names onto's split
#   had just pushed apart.  Read the certificate: the chain joining
#   the excluded pair goes straight through masthead.png, the name
#   that lost its antecedent.  Open problem 2's rule, arrived at by
#   way of open problem 6.
#
# Whether a sound refinement exists is genuinely open, and note what
# is NOT available to one here: identical content, and no positional
# evidence of any kind.  The conservative degrade at least fails
# loudly instead of pairing the names out of the air.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: one-rename/
      # one picture, four names.
      10-100: banner.png = img-v1
      10-100: header.png
      10-100: splash.png
      10-100: hero.png
    3-100: two-renames/
      20-100: banner.png = img-v1
      20-100: header.png
      20-100: splash.png
      20-100: hero.png

tree onto
  # The same un-sharing in both folders: hero.png gets its own dnode.
  1-1: /
    2-100: one-rename/
      10-100: banner.png = img-v1
      10-100: header.png
      10-100: splash.png
      210-200: hero.png = img-v1
    3-100: two-renames/
      20-100: banner.png = img-v1
      20-100: header.png
      20-100: splash.png
      220-200: hero.png = img-v1

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: one-rename/
      # one rename: succession has exactly one way to read it.
      10-100: masthead.png = img-v1
      10-100: header.png
      10-100: splash.png
      10-100: hero.png
    3-100: two-renames/
      # two renames at once: succession has two ways and takes neither.
      20-100: masthead.png = img-v1
      20-100: topbar.png
      20-100: splash.png
      20-100: hero.png

# --- expected result, generated by gen-expected.js ---
expect conflict pooling
expect quarantined /two-renames/banner.png
expect quarantined /two-renames/header.png
expect quarantined /two-renames/hero.png
expect quarantined /two-renames/splash.png

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: one-rename
      10-100: header.png = img-v1
      210-200: hero.png = img-v1
      10-100: masthead.png
      10-100: splash.png
    3-100: two-renames
      20-100: banner.png = img-v1
      20-100: header.png
      220-200: hero.png = img-v1
      20-100: splash.png
